# ENVIRONMENT — read before running any command

These are verified facts about this machine, not suggestions. Checking them costs nothing;
guessing costs a reinstall loop or a hung command.

## Start here: `tasks.py`

Every routine operation has one canonical form. **Use it instead of composing your own
invocation** — the runner always re-launches under the venv interpreter, so a task cannot land
on the wrong Python regardless of shell or activation state.

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python.exe tasks.py doctor    # FIRST COMMAND OF ANY FRESH SESSION
.venv/Scripts/python.exe tasks.py test      # full suite (206 tests, ~11 s)
.venv/Scripts/python.exe tasks.py test sim  # one lane: livewatch | gui | sim | flight
.venv/Scripts/python.exe tasks.py verify    # ELF-vs-flash check (needs the SWD probe)
.venv/Scripts/python.exe tasks.py graph     # rebuild the graphify code graph
.venv/Scripts/python.exe tasks.py freeze    # regenerate requirements.txt
```

`doctor` answers, in one command, everything below this line — which interpreter, which
packages, whether git will hang, whether the probe is free. Run it before asking the operator
anything about the environment. It exits non-zero only on real problems; an absent probe is
reported as a note, since it blocks `verify` and nothing else.

## Python — always use the venv interpreter explicitly

**There are three Pythons on PATH.** The venv is *usually* auto-activated but you cannot rely
on it:

```text
.venv/Scripts/python          <- has every dependency   (auto-activated, but not guaranteed)
~/AppData/.../Python311/python  <- bare, no deps
~/AppData/.../Python313/python  <- bare, no deps
```

If you run bare `python` in a shell where the venv did not activate, you land on **3.11 with no
dependencies**, get `ModuleNotFoundError`, and "fix" it by installing into the wrong
interpreter. That is the reinstall loop. Avoid it by never writing bare `python`:

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m ground_station.livewatch verify
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest -q ground_station/livewatch/tests
```

Verified working: `.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m ground_station.livewatch manifests` from the
repo root. Module invocation needs the repo root as CWD.

`pytest.ini` pins `testpaths`, so a bare `pytest` from the repo root now collects all four
lanes (206 tests) instead of inferring rootdir per-invocation.

## Packages — check before you install

`requirements.txt` is a full pinned freeze of the venv (57 packages). **Read it before running
any install.** Already present, current versions:

| Package | Version | | Package | Version |
|---------|---------|-|---------|---------|
| pyocd | 0.45.1 | | numpy | 2.4.4 |
| pyserial | 3.5 | | scipy | 1.18.0 |
| pytest | 9.0.3 | | matplotlib | 3.10.8 |
| PyYAML | 6.0.3 | | | |

Rules:

- **Never** run `pip install` for something already in `requirements.txt`. Check first.
- If something is genuinely missing, install it into the venv explicitly
  (`.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install X`), then regenerate the freeze:
  `.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip freeze > requirements.txt`.
- Never `pip install --user`, never install into a system Python, never `sudo`.
- A `ModuleNotFoundError` is far more likely to be *the wrong interpreter* than a missing
  package. Check `sys.executable` before installing anything.

## Git — the pager is the thing that hangs you

`core.pager` is now set to `cat` **in this repo**. Before that, `git log` / `git diff` / `git
show` opened a pager and blocked until the tool timed out — that was the git slowness, not the
repo (156 MiB packed, `git status` runs in 0.1 s).

Belt and braces, since the setting is repo-local and won't follow a fresh clone or worktree:

```bash
git --no-pager log --oneline -20
git --no-pager diff --stat
```

Other git notes for this repo:

- Interactive flags (`-i`) do not work here. No `rebase -i`, no `add -i`.
- The tree carries large build artifacts; prefer `--stat` or explicit paths over bare
  `git diff` on the whole tree.
- `git stash` is **repo-level and shared across worktrees**. Do not use it as a checkpoint
  when more than one agent may be running. Use a scratch-branch commit instead.
- **An hourly scheduled task (`UAV-autopush`) pushes the current branch to origin.** So a
  branch may reach the remote without anyone running `git push` — that is expected, not a
  sign someone else pushed. It only ever pushes existing commits: it never commits, stages,
  force-pushes, or touches the working tree. Log: `.agent_state/autopush.log`.
  Remove with `schtasks /Delete /TN "UAV-autopush" /F`.
- Because commits reach the remote within the hour, **do not amend or rebase a commit that is
  more than an hour old** without checking `git log @{u}..` first.

## Shell — two shells, two syntaxes, do not mix

PowerShell is primary; Git Bash is also available. Each tool call picks one — write that
shell's syntax and do not blend them.

PowerShell (Windows PowerShell 5.1) gotchas that cost the most time here:

- No `&&` or `||`. Use `A; if ($?) { B }`.
- No ternary, no `??`, no `?.`.
- `head`/`tail`/`which`/`touch` do not exist — `Select-Object -First N`, `-Last N`,
  `(Get-Command x).Source`.
- Do not redirect a native `.exe`'s stderr with `2>&1`; 5.1 wraps it in an ErrorRecord and
  reports failure on exit code 0.

Paths on this machine **contain spaces** (`FreeRTOS---Six_Degrees_of_Freedom _Adaptive_controller`
— note the space before `_Adaptive`). Always quote paths. This bites nearly every fresh session.

## When a command fails

Diagnose before escalating. The three failures that account for most of it:

1. Wrong Python interpreter (see above).
2. Unquoted path with a space.
3. Wrong shell's syntax.

None of these are reasons to stop the task or ask the operator for help — fix and retry. See
`.cursor/rules/hardware-safety.mdc` for the (short) list of things that genuinely require
stopping.
